\section*{Global weak normalization implies strong normalization in pure type systems}

\subsection{OpenAI's claim}
OpenAI presents the following claims relevant to this question, with manuscript numbers following paper order in Sources. In manuscript 1 (family 245; 2026-09-25), the asserted result is that weak beta normalization of an arbitrary pure type system implies strong beta normalization across all legal expressions and contexts, including reduction in annotations.

\subsection{Outline of the argument}
For manuscript 1 (family 245; 2026-09-25), a finite counterexample reduces to finitely many sorts and rules. Sets of possible typing sorts replace unique types in nonfunctional systems; substitution-stable observation candidates and typed data channels turn an infinite reduction into an obstruction to any normal form.

\subsection{Scope relative to this problem}
The result supplies the conclusion in this problem with its displayed hypotheses and quantifiers. The broader statements and applications in the manuscript are kept separate from the particular question above. The supporting result is manuscript 1 (family 245; 2026-09-25).

\subsection{Formal verification}
For manuscript 1 (family 245; 2026-09-25), OpenAI lists Lean statements for this assertion: weak beta normalization of an arbitrary pure type system implies strong beta normalization across all legal expressions and contexts, including reduction in annotations.

The central assertion is within the documented formal scope; the additional applications retain their own scope limits.

The linked family Comparator checks are \texttt{TypeSystemNormalization.lean}.

Their named declarations include \texttt{OAI.PureTypeSystem.weak\_implies\_strong}.

This site has not executed the Lean code. Described statement coverage is not an independent proof check.

\subsection{Status caveat}
The manuscripts were produced by an internal OpenAI model. OpenAI cautions that results without formalization can contain mistakes. This site has not independently checked the proofs; the label records the scope of OpenAI's claim.

